# Automation KQL Queries

## Overview

Although Microsoft Sentinel Automation Rules are configured through the Automation workspace rather than Kusto Query Language (KQL), KQL remains an important tool for validating incidents that trigger automation and for investigating security events after automated actions have been applied.

The following queries are useful during post-incident investigation and automation validation.

---

# Query 1 – View Recent Incidents

```kusto
SecurityIncident
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
```

### Purpose

Displays the most recently created Microsoft Sentinel incidents.

---

# Query 2 – View High Severity Incidents

```kusto
SecurityIncident
| where Severity =~ "High"
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
```

### Purpose

Returns only High severity incidents.

This query is useful because the Automation Rule created during this implementation executes only for High severity incidents.

---

# Query 3 – View Incident Status

```kusto
SecurityIncident
| project TimeGenerated,
          IncidentNumber,
          Title,
          Severity,
          Status,
          Owner
| sort by TimeGenerated desc
```

### Purpose

Displays important incident information including severity, current status, and assigned owner.

---

# Query 4 – Count Incidents by Severity

```kusto
SecurityIncident
| summarize TotalIncidents=count() by Severity
| sort by TotalIncidents desc
```

### Purpose

Provides a summary of incidents grouped by severity level.

---

# Notes

These queries support investigation and validation after automation has been configured. While Automation Rules themselves are not created with KQL, KQL enables analysts to verify that qualifying incidents exist and to investigate incidents processed by automated workflows.

Before using additional queries, it is recommended to verify the available schema in your Microsoft Sentinel workspace, as table structures may vary between environments.
